## 5. Drift detection on prediction quality (when has the win model gone stale?)

| Technique | What it does | Trade-offs | Recommendation here |
|---|---|---|---|
| Page-Hinkley test | Cumulative deviation of a stream metric vs its running mean; alarms on sustained shift | ~20 lines, stdlib; tunable sensitivity (delta/gamma); designed for gradual drift | **First choice.** Run on the stream of win-model calibration errors: `error = p_pred - outcome` (loss -> +p_pred, win -> -(1-p_pred); mean 0 iff calibrated). [R4 correction - round 1-3 wrote `1-p` for losses / `-p` for wins, whose mean is `1-p-q`, i.e. -0.10 at p=q=0.55: NOT zero-mean. See R4.4-F.] |
| DDM (Drift Detection Method) | Binomial error-rate bounds (warning/drift at 2/3 std) | Simple; assumes binary error stream | Second choice; same data as P-H |
| ADWIN | Adaptive windowing, theoretical guarantees, no params | Real implementation is nontrivial; `river` library has it | Only if `river` is ever allowed as a dependency |
| Rolling calibration plot | Predicted win prob vs realized win rate by decile, last N tenders | No alarm logic, but humans read it instantly; also detects overconfidence | **Put in the generated report** - it doubles as the "should we switch models" evidence |
| Data drift on inputs (competitor count, price ratios) | Input distribution shift as leading indicator | Needs distribution comparisons (PSI/KS) | Cheap version: track min/median/max of bid-ratio per quarter in the report |

