### R2.2 Deepened: catastrophic forgetting for LoRA/PEFT (replay vs EWC vs rebase)

Primary sources (all fetched today):

- **Rebase (retrain adapter from base on all data) is a legitimate CL strategy, not a cop-out.** "Continual Learning with Low Rank Adaptation" (Amazon, arXiv 2311.17601 - same paper as the round-1 cdn.amazon.science PDF, confirmed) shows simply retraining LoRA per domain outperforms prompt-tuning-style continual methods.
- **Replay wins when memory is cheap - and ours is.** "Forget Forgetting: Continual Learning in a World of Abundant Memory" (arXiv 2502.07274): when storage is abundant but GPU time is the bottleneck, replay-based mitigation dominates and the residual challenge shifts from stability to *plasticity*. The `uploads/lora_data/` corpus is exactly this regime: keep every upload forever, always include prior data. Corroborated by "Dynamic Proxy-Mixing ..." (arXiv 2606.00400): "Replay is the standard mitigation, but fixed replay ratios are inherently limited" (optimal mix varies) - i.e. replay yes, but tune the old:new ratio rather than hard-coding 50:50.
- **Subspace/isolation methods (InfLoRA arXiv 2404.00228, O-LoRA, Gated-LoRA arXiv 2505.15424, CLoRA arXiv 2507.19887, MuLoRA arXiv 2610.02283)** preserve old tasks without storing data by constraining updates to (near-)orthogonal low-rank subspaces. Cost: training-time complexity, merge/serving complexity (S-LoRA arXiv 2407.00066 exists precisely because multi-adapter serving is nontrivial). Verdict unchanged: **not for 3 tasks x monthly retrain.**
- **EWC/L2-SP regularisation:** no new evidence in favor at small scale; remains tuning-sensitive (Fisher estimation noise on tiny datasets). Round-1 "overkill; note for later" stands.

**Practical guidance for this repo (unchanged in conclusion, now evidence-backed):**
1. Default: full rebase from base on cumulative corpus each retrain (anti-forgetting by construction).
2. If incremental training from the previous adapter is ever introduced: replay mix with *tuned* ratio (start 70% old / 30% new; grid-search the ratio on the golden set), never train on the new batch alone.
3. The golden-set regression gate (R2.4) is the safety net independent of which of the two above is used.

